  "Question": "If \\(1-\\frac4x+\\frac4{x^2}=0,\\) then \\(\\frac2x\\) equals",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(-1\\)",
    "B": "\\(1\\)",
    "C": "\\(2\\)",
    "D": "\\(-1\\) or \\(2\\)",
    "E": "\\(-1\\) or \\(-2\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "B",
  "Solution": "The denominators require \\(x\\ne0\\). The coefficients suggest rewriting the left side as a perfect square:\n\\[\n1-\\frac4x+\\frac4{x^2}=\\left(1-\\frac2x\\right)^2.\n\\]\nA square equals zero only when its base equals zero. Therefore \\(1-\\frac2x=0\\), giving \\(\\frac2x=1\\). The corresponding value \\(x=2\\) is nonzero and satisfies the original equation. The answer is \\(1\\), choice B.",
